    "title": "Increasing police presence at the Oxford Apartments to reduce crime - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Tyler Louder ,\n1 hours ago RAPID CITY, S.D. (KELO) — As the Rapid City Police Department investigates the city’s latest homicide, officers are also increasing their visibility in the neighborhood where it happened.\nSouth Dakota officials react to Charlie Kirk shooting Following recent violent crimes, people who live in the Oxford Apartments don’t feel safe.\n“Strangers in and out, calling the police because people are sleeping in the hallways and the laundry room, people trying to open our door and we’ve never really felt safe here.” Oxford Resident Tia Ghost said.\nRapid City Police are taking steps to make residents in the area feel safe.\n“We’re having our officers go through there approximately once an hour, sometimes more. So yesterday, between like four thirty in the afternoon and eight in the morning, we were there 16 times and 13 were just us proactively,” RCPD Lieutenant Tim Doyle said.\nBy having a presence here more often, they’re able to mitigate some of those problems by having a zero-tolerance policy. They’ve made several arrests following the tragedy that took place this week.\nThey’ve arrested people for everything from drugs and assaults to trespassing and active warrants.\n“In an apartment complex there’s more people coming and going, there’s more people to stop and identify and we do have a tenant list. If they’re not on that list and they’re on the property, they need to go. That’s worked so far and again, you have to take a zero-tolerance approach with this kind of situation,” Doyle said.\nSo far, police have made two arrests in connection with this week’s murder.\nThe Rapid City Police Department is also attempting to work with the building management team to address security and find other solutions to the issues inside.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KELOLAND.com.",
